#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""msbctl — manage the registered microsandbox dev environments on this machine.

    msbctl                        no arguments: a menu
    msbctl status                 one block per sandbox (the menu sidebar)
    msbctl add [dir]              register a project (wizard); does not start
    msbctl ls [--refresh]         one line per sandbox
    msbctl show <name>            everything about one, including versions
    msbctl start|stop <name>…         repeatable: acts on each in turn
    msbctl rebuild <name>         destroy and recreate; keeps ~/.claude state
    msbctl purge <name>           destroy, delete state, secret and registration
    msbctl rename <name> <new>    new name; recreates it, everything else moves along
    msbctl move <name> <dir>      point it at another project folder (then rebuild)
    msbctl update <name>… [-c what…] update claude / gh / gitleaks / bun / apt in place
    msbctl shell <name>           interactive shell, ssh-agent wired up
    msbctl keys <name>            choose which host ssh keys it can use
    msbctl config                 the merged configuration, with where each value is from
    msbctl setup                  your machine-level settings, as a menu (first run walks you through)
    msbctl self-update            upgrade a packaged install to the latest release
    msbctl edit <name>            edit an existing sandbox: a menu of the wizard's sections
    msbctl resize <name>          change cpus / memory / root disk, keeping its state
    msbctl cache <name> [ls|clear [key]]   the package-cache disks
    msbctl allow <name> host…     add egress rules (then rebuild to apply)
    msbctl observe <name> [on|off|report]   stop blocking and record what is reached
    msbctl secret add|rm|ls <name>   extra tokens the sandbox can use but not see
    msbctl mount <name> SRC[:DEST][:ro|rw]   add an extra host folder (then rebuild)
    msbctl port <name> [port…] [--rm port]   publish a host port to a server inside (then rebuild)
    msbctl exec <name> [flags] -- cmd…   shell and exec pass msb exec's flags on
    msbctl code <name>            open the editor on the project, on the host
    msbctl reclaim <name>         give the guest's page cache back to the host

Inside a registered project folder the name can be left out where it is the
only argument (`msbctl shell`, `msbctl stop`), and `.` stands for it where more
follows (`msbctl exec . make`, `msbctl allow . example.com`); `msbctl exec --
cmd` works too. shell and exec start in the same subfolder of the project
inside the sandbox (/work, or the host's own path: `guest_path`).

Runs on the HOST. Needs /dev/kvm, so not from inside a container.

DEPENDENCIES ARE DELIBERATELY NONE — Python 3.11+ stdlib and the `msb` binary,
so this has to still start after a year of not being touched: a sandbox manager
that is itself broken by a dependency upgrade is worse than no sandbox manager.

CONFIGURATION IS FOUR FILES, resolved in this order (later wins):

    <repo>/defaults.toml                   shipped: rule groups, pins, baselines
    ~/.config/msb/config.toml              yours: only what differs from that
    <project>/.msb/sandbox.toml            committed, portable, per project
    ~/.config/msb/sandboxes/<name>.toml    machine-local: paths, overrides

Registry entries are written WHOLE from templates/ and only ever read back with
tomllib, so there is no TOML serializer here to rot. The one exception is the
[versions] table, which `update` rewrites — see write_version_override().
"""

